<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Winchester</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Venta Belgarum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Winchester is a cathedral city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venta-belgarum">Venta Belgarum</a> it was the Roman capital of the Belgae, and it later became the chief royal seat of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, and an early capital of England. Its cathedral, begun in 1079, is one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winchester.jpg" alt="Winchester" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (CC BY-SA 2.0) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winchester_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Winchester lies on the River Itchen at the western end of the South Downs National Park, 60 miles (97 km) south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. It is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillforts, Oram's Arbour, St. Catherine's Hill and Worthy Down, stand nearby, and in the Late Iron Age an oppidum grew up that became an important centre of the British Belgae tribe. It remains unclear how the Belgae came to control it. Caesar recorded that they crossed the Channel as raiders, though recent studies favour gradual change through trade over migration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Roman conquest the town became the capital of the Belgae as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venta-belgarum">Venta Belgarum</a>. It was at first less important than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calleva-atrebatum">Silchester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a> but eclipsed both by the latter half of the second century, and at the start of the third century it was given stone walls. It then covered 144 acres (58 ha), among the largest towns in Roman Britain, but declined from the later fourth century. Urban life continued into the mid fifth century, and the Christian cemeteries stayed in use.</p><p>In Old English it was Wintanceaster. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenwalh-of-wessex">Cenwalh of Wessex</a> built the Church of St Peter and St Paul, later the Old Minster, in 648, and it became a cathedral in the 660s when the West Saxon see moved from Dorchester on Thames. In the late 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> replaced the Roman street plan with a new grid for better defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, and the city's first mint seems to date from then. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was an important centre of the wool trade before slowly declining. A fire in 1141 came during the Rout of Winchester, and in the 14th century William of Wykeham, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>, rebuilt much of the cathedral and founded Winchester College.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Winchester Cathedral, begun in 1079, remains the longest Gothic cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and holds the burials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Egbert of Wessex</a>, of Canute and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a>. It was once a pilgrimage centre, housing the shrine of Saint Swithun. The Great Hall of the castle, rebuilt between 1222 and 1235, has displayed King Arthur's Round Table since at least 1463; the table dates from the 13th century and was painted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> in 1522. The built-up area had 48,478 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The city is also home to the University of Winchester, and Winchester College is the oldest public school in the United Kingdom still using its original buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Winchester” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventh century, under pressure from Mercia in the Thames valley, the kingdom’s centre shifted south and west to Hampshire and Wiltshire, and <b>Winchester</b> became its principal ecclesiastical and royal centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The prolific Ælfric of Eynsham, ‘the Grammarian’, wrote in the late <b>Winchester</b> standard.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Smith reprinted Haswell's words and gave a second version, presumably collected by herself, said to be sung for hoisting the topsail yards, in which a sailor comes home to England from Hong Kong and meets a girl on &quot;<b>Winchester</b> Street&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/blow-the-man-down">Blow the Man Down</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of Æthelred the Unready and Emma of Normandy, was crowned king of the English in <b>Winchester</b> Cathedral, having succeeded his half-brother Harthacnut in 1042.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bishop Walkelin's Romanesque cathedral at <b>Winchester</b> was consecrated in the presence of bishops and abbots.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-8">8 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Winchester</b> Cathedral Henry, eldest surviving son of Henry II, was crowned King of England a second time, now with his wife Margaret of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
